  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 440 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe MCS that tracked across southern Nebraska earlier in the night \nhas weakened considerably since 3am, with its remnants going through \nthe MO River Valley as of 840z. Additional non-severe storms have \ndeveloped within the WAA regime upstream in western Nebraska and \neastern SD. These are the storms that are progged to move across \nNebraska and eventually enter northern KS during the mid to late \nmorning hours. There are varying ideas among CAMs on how far south \nthis activity will make it into the area, and some solutions hang \nonto light showery activity in northeast KS even into the afternoon. \nThis leads to questions on where any outflow boundary will set up, \nas well as how much the atmosphere will be able to destabilize, \nespecially if clouds and rain hang around longer. Consensus appears \nto place a boundary near the I-70 corridor, perhaps due to \ndifferential heating creating an effective warm front. Soundings \ncontinue to show a lot of inhibition, whether that's because of \nbetter mixing south of the boundary leading to warmer temperatures \nand lower dew points, or because of capping north of the boundary. \nEither way, it appears as though there needs to be a stronger source \nof lift to overcome the inhibition and develop stronger storms, and \nthe cold front seems to be that necessary forcing. Generally \nspeaking, models struggle to develop stronger convection until the \ncold front moves through later in the evening. Location of \ninitiation is still up in the air, as models still haven't shown \nmuch consistency on where storms will develop. Recent runs are now \ntrying to initiate closer to Hwy 36, which would theoretically be \nwithin the rain-cooled air, and the timing would suggest it's \nrelated to the 850mb boundary. The environment for these elevated \nstorms would still be favorable for mainly damaging wind with DCAPE \nover 1000 J/kg with perhaps some large hail. Skinny CAPE profiles \nand Pwat near 90th percentile also point to efficient rainfall \nproducers, so heavy rain would be a potential concern as well. While \nit's uncertain how much coverage there will be (particularly severe \ncoverage), storms should generally weaken through the night. Some \nrain may linger into Wednesday morning before finally moving out by \nmidday.\n\nAs for later in the day Wednesday, the wave within the northwest \nflow aloft has shifted east in the latest model runs, favoring storm \nchances to the south and east of the area. Some low PoPs remain in \neast central KS with higher chances outside of the forecast area. \nTemperatures are cooler Wednesday behind the front with highs in the \n80s, perhaps some upper 70s in northern KS. A gradual warming trend \noccurs the rest of the week with temperatures increasing a couple \ndegrees each day. We remain in a northwest flow pattern aloft with \nan amplifying ridge to our west and a deep trough to our east. \nTiming and placement of embedded waves within this pattern are \ntypically hard to predict several days out, so low chance PoPs \nappear periodically towards the end of the week.",
